          pathway: 'Documentary',
          teacher_guidance:
            'Examines the brutal, satirical paintings of Otto Dix and George Grosz (Neue Sachlichkeit), Berlin cabaret satire, and the growing chasm between cosmopolitan Berlin and the conservative countryside. Recommended Watch Window: 28:00–36:00 • Lesson Phase: High-Attainer Stretch & Visual Art Analysis. Tone Alert: Rich cultural documentary featuring artwork depicting wounded war veterans.',
          viewing_task:
            '<ol><li><strong>[29:30] Recall:</strong> Which two famous "New Objectivity" (*Neue Sachlichkeit*) painters depicted disfigured WWI amputees begging outside luxury Berlin nightclubs?</li><li><strong>[32:15] Explain:</strong> Why did Berlin become celebrated worldwide as the cultural capital of tolerance, cabaret satire, and avant-garde freedom during the late 1920s?</li><li><strong>[34:40] Challenge:</strong> How did right-wing newspapers and the Nazi Party exploit public disgust with Berlin\'s modern culture to attract conservative, religious voters?</li></ol>',
          model_answer:
            '<ol><li><strong>Recall:</strong> Otto Dix and George Grosz.</li><li><strong>Explain:</strong> The abolition of imperial censorship under Article 118 unleashed an explosion of artistic experimentation. Berlin became a haven for playwrights (Bertolt Brecht), scientists (Albert Einstein), jazz musicians, and pioneering sexologists, offering unprecedented personal freedom.</li><li><strong>Challenge:</strong> Rural and small-town Germans viewed Berlin as a cesspool of moral depravity and unpatriotic decadence. The visual art of Grosz and Dix—which stripped away patriotic myths of wartime heroism—infuriated military veterans, allowing Hitler to present National Socialism as a holy crusade to cleanse German culture of degenerate filth.</li></ol>',
