              id: 'weimar_l4_source_b',
              letter: 'B',
              title: 'Source B: Alice Rühle-Gerstel, "The New Woman" (Die neue Frau, 1927)',
              type: 'written',
              text: 'The "New Woman" stands before us: bobbed hair, slender silhouette, smoking a cigarette in a public café, earning her own wages as a typist, shop assistant, or schoolmistress. Under Article 109 of the Weimar Constitution, she possesses equal civil rights and exercises the vote alongside men. She enjoys an unprecedented measure of economic independence and personal mobility, riding bicycles and dancing the Charleston until midnight. Yet this liberation is deeply ambiguous. Employers pay her barely sixty percent of male wages, dismissing her the moment she marries. When she returns from the office, the traditional domestic duties—cooking, scrubbing, caring for children—still await her alone. Society celebrates the fashionable illusion of freedom, but conservative men and church elders condemn her as selfish and unfeminine.',
              caption:
                'Contemporary essay by social commentator Alice Rühle-Gerstel in Die literarische Welt (1927), analysing the real versus superficial emancipation of German women.',
              desc: "Contemporary analytical essay on women's emancipation in Weimar Germany by Alice Rühle-Gerstel, Berlin, September 1927.",
