              context:
                'Contemporary essay by Alice Rühle-Gerstel published in Die literarische Welt in September 1927.',
              question_badge: 'How Useful (Paper 3 Q3a)',
              question_type: 'How Useful (Paper 3 Q3a)',
              enquiry_question:
                'How useful is Source B for an enquiry into the extent to which the lives of German women changed during the Weimar Republic?',
              model_answer:
                '<p><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source B is extremely useful because it provides a nuanced contemporary assessment of female emancipation under the Weimar Republic. Rühle-Gerstel highlights the tangible freedoms enjoyed by the urban "New Woman" (<em>Neue Frau</em>), including short bobbed hair, personal mobility, voting rights under Article 109 of the Constitution, and employment in white-collar sectors such as clerical work and retail. Crucially, however, she exposes the limits of this emancipation: women earned approximately 33% less than men, faced the "marriage bar" (dismissal upon marriage), and still carried the sole burden of unassisted domestic housework alongside intense hostility from traditionalists.</p><p><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> The utility is further strengthened by Rühle-Gerstel’s position as a leading feminist psychologist writing in a respected cultural journal (<em>Die literarische Welt</em>) in 1927. Her purpose was not to produce shallow commercial propaganda or hostile anti-feminist panic, but to critically evaluate the lived reality of working women behind the fashionable media façade. While it offers deep insight into the contradictions of Weimar modernization, its focus is primarily on young, urban, middle-class office workers in major cities like Berlin, making it less representative of rural, working-class mothers or agrarian communities where traditional patriarchal roles remained completely unchallenged.</p>',
