              model_answer:
                '<p><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source E is exceptionally useful because it provides direct visual evidence of the fierce right-wing backlash against Weimar liberalism during the 1928 Reichstag election campaign. The DNVP used stark imagery to warn that the republic was destroying Germany\'s traditional Christian morals through "Cultural Bolshevism", attacking modern art, female independence, cabaret, and American jazz as degenerate imports designed to undermine the Volk. This aligns precisely with historical knowledge: conservative elites, agrarian landowners, and the Protestant middle class felt deeply alienated by Berlin’s cosmopolitan culture, which they blamed on socialist politicians and Jewish intellectuals.</p><p><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> As official propaganda produced by the DNVP (the largest conservative nationalist party in the Reichstag), the source is invaluable for revealing the political strategy and genuine cultural anxieties of right-wing voters. Its purpose was to exploit moral panic and mobilize rural, religious, and traditionalist voters against the Weimar coalition. While the source cannot provide an objective or balanced reflection of Weimar cultural achievements—deliberately presenting modern art and emancipation as sinister subversion—this very subjectivity makes it uniquely valuable for demonstrating why cultural experimentation deeply polarized German society and fueled support for authoritarian nationalism.</p>',
