          title:
            'Bloodshed at the Feldherrnhalle: Bavarian Police Fire and the Collapse of the Coup (9 Nov 1923)',
          act: 2,
          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> Hitler\'s triumph evaporated overnight. Once released from the beer hall, Kahr and Lossow promptly repudiated their coerced pledges and alerted the Bavarian state police and army garrisons. Meanwhile, Ernst Röhm\'s SA units were trapped inside the War Ministry by regular troops. Desperate to regain the initiative, Hitler and Ludendorff led 2,000 armed Nazis on a march through central Munich at midday on 9 November, hoping public acclaim and Ludendorff’s prestige would convince the police to defect.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> As the column approached the <em>Residenzstrasse</em> near the Feldherrnhalle monument, they encountered a cordon of one hundred armed Bavarian state police. Gunfire erupted: sixteen Nazis and four police officers were killed in minutes. Ludendorff marched straight through the police line unharmed and was arrested, while Hitler dislocated his shoulder diving for cover and fled in a waiting car. Two days later, Hitler was arrested hiding in the attic of Ernst Hanfstaengl\'s villa outside Munich; the armed putsch had ended in humiliating disaster.</p>',
