              intro:
                "Extracts from Chancellor Heinrich Brüning's emergency decree signed by President Paul von Hindenburg under Article 48 on 8 December 1931.",
              hinge_question:
                'Why did Chancellor Brüning\'s ruthless austerity measures earn him the hated title of the "Hunger Chancellor" (Hungerkanzler)?',
              source_context:
                'Between 1930 and 1932, Centre Party Chancellor Heinrich Brüning bypassed the deadlocked Reichstag, using Hindenburg\'s Article 48 emergency powers to slash state spending, unemployment benefits, and public sector salaries. **Hinge Question:** Why did Chancellor Brüning\'s ruthless austerity measures earn him the hated title of the "Hunger Chancellor" (Hungerkanzler)?',
              context:
                "Official text of Chancellor Heinrich Brüning's emergency decree enacted under Article 48 in December 1931.",
              question_badge: 'How Useful (Paper 3 Q3a)',
              question_type: 'How Useful (Paper 3 Q3a)',
              enquiry_question:
                'How useful is Source B for an enquiry into the economic policies of Chancellor Heinrich Brüning and their impact on public faith in Weimar democracy?',
              model_answer:
                '<p><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source B is extremely useful because it provides documentary proof of the deflationary austerity policies that alienated millions of Germans from the Weimar Republic between 1930 and 1932. Brüning slashed civil service pay, war pensions, and unemployment benefits by 9% to 12% at the very moment when winter poverty was at its absolute worst. This aligns with contextual knowledge: Brüning pursued this harsh policy partly to demonstrate to Britain and France that Germany was completely incapable of paying reparations (which were indeed cancelled at Lausanne in 1932), but the domestic consequence was catastrophic, driving millions of impoverished workers and lower-middle-class families into the arms of the political extremes (KPD and NSDAP).</p><p><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> The utility is reinforced by its status as an official state statute published in the <em>Reichsgesetzblatt</em>, enacted under Article 48 by presidential signature rather than parliamentary debate. This provenance directly illustrates the erosion of German parliamentary democracy: because the Reichstag coalition had collapsed in March 1930 over unemployment contributions, Brüning governed entirely through authoritarian decrees. However, the legal decree cannot show the emotional fury, breadlines, or street protests that erupted across German cities in response, making it essential to cross-reference with personal memoirs and election results to assess the full political backlash.</p>',
