              desc: 'Extract from Melita Maschmann, Fazit: Kein Rechtfertigungsversuch (Account Rendered), Deutsche Verlags-Anstalt, Stuttgart, 1963.',
              provenance: 'Melita Maschmann, Account Rendered, London: Abelard-Schuman, 1964.',
              citation: 'German Literature Archive (Deutsches Literaturarchiv), Marbach.',
              intro:
                'From the memoir of Melita Maschmann, describing her family’s growing sympathy for Adolf Hitler in Berlin during the winter of 1931–1932.',
              hinge_question:
                "Why was the intense fear of a Communist revolution the primary factor that drove Germany's middle class (Mittelstand) to support Adolf Hitler?",
              source_context:
                "Written by a middle-class Berlin teenager who joined the Hitler Youth movement in 1932, Maschmann's memoir provides deep insight into why respectable German families turned to Hitler out of dread of Communism and economic ruin. **Hinge Question:** Why was the intense fear of a Communist revolution the primary factor that drove Germany's middle class (Mittelstand) to support Adolf Hitler?",
              context:
                "Memoir by Melita Maschmann describing her Berlin middle-class family's reaction to the Great Depression in 1931–1932.",
              question_badge: 'How Useful (Paper 3 Q3a)',
              question_type: 'How Useful (Paper 3 Q3a)',
              enquiry_question:
                'How useful is Source E for an enquiry into why middle-class Germans were attracted to National Socialism during the Great Depression?',
              model_answer:
                "<p><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source E is exceptionally useful because it provides an authentic, psychological explanation of why respectable middle-class Germans (<em>Mittelstand</em>) abandoned traditional conservative parties for Hitler in 1931–1932. Maschmann vividly highlights the overriding dread of a violent Bolshevik revolution, fuelled by daily street clashes between Communists and the SA in residential Berlin. Furthermore, she reveals how the middle class felt economically humiliated by the Depression and disgusted by the perceived cowardice of democratic politicians, making Hitler’s uncompromising message of national discipline, anti-communism, and patriotic renewal overwhelmingly attractive to young people.</p><p><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> The utility is further enriched by Maschmann's position as an educated contemporary participant who lived through the radicalization of Berlin first-hand and subsequently joined the Nazi youth movement. Writing in retrospect to honestly explain how ordinary, decent Germans were seduced by fascism, her testimony provides rare emotional insight into the moral vacuum of late Weimar Germany. However, its utility is limited because it is an autobiographical memoir written decades later, which may unconsciously exaggerate her family's idealism to soften their complicity in supporting a violent regime; nonetheless, it remains invaluable for showing how anti-Marxist fear and charismatic populism converged to create Hitler's mass electoral base.</p>",
