          type: 'written',
          text: 'My parents belonged to the conservative Berlin middle class. By the winter of 1931, our dinner table conversations were dominated by one terrifying fear: Communist revolution. Every week there were bloody clashes outside our apartment between the Red Front and the brownshirts. We saw our neighbours—respectable shopkeepers and civil servants—lose their savings and their dignity, forced to pawn their heirlooms. We felt that the Weimar politicians were weak, selfish men who talked endlessly in the Reichstag while Germany sank into the abyss. When my brother and I heard Hitler speak, it was as if someone had opened a window in a suffocating room. He spoke with a burning conviction that electrified us. He did not offer us comfortable compromises; he demanded self-sacrifice for the nation. We believed that he alone possessed the iron will to stop the Bolsheviks and restore our fatherland to greatness.',
