          title:
            'Gleichschaltung: Smashing the Trade Unions and Banning All Rival Political Parties (May–Jul 1933)',
          act: 3,
          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> Armed with the Enabling Act, Hitler launched Gleichschaltung (coordination), forcing every German institution under Nazi control. On 2 May 1933, the day after celebrating May Day as a national holiday, SA and SS squads raided trade union offices across Germany, arresting union leaders and seizing bank assets. In their place, Robert Ley established the German Labour Front (DAF), a state body that outlawed strikes and controlled factory wages and discipline.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> The elimination of rival political parties followed with ruthless speed. On 22 June, the brave SPD was formally outlawed as hostile to the state, its assets confiscated and deputies sent to concentration camps. Intimidated by Nazi terror, the nationalist DNVP, liberal DVP, and Catholic Centre Party dissolved themselves over the following weeks. On 14 July 1933, the Law Against the Establishment of Parties declared the NSDAP Germany\'s sole legal political party.</p>',
