          title:
            'The Night of the Long Knives: Purging Ernst Röhm and the SA Leadership (30 Jun 1934)',
          act: 4,
          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> By mid-1934, Hitler faced a serious internal power struggle. Ernst Röhm, commander of the three-million-strong SA, demanded a \'second revolution\' to redistribute wealth and absorb the aristocratic regular army (Reichswehr) into his brownshirt militia. The traditional officer corps and Defence Minister Werner von Blomberg were horrified, warning Hitler that President Hindenburg would declare martial law unless the unruly SA was brought under control.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> Prompted by fabricated dossiers of an impending SA coup compiled by Heinrich Himmler and Reinhard Heydrich, Hitler struck on 30 June 1934, known as the Night of the Long Knives. SS execution squads arrested Röhm and senior SA commanders at a Bavarian resort, shooting them without trial. In Berlin, hit squads assassinated former Chancellor Kurt von Schleicher and prominent dissident Gregor Strasser, eliminating internal party radicalism and securing the military\'s gratitude.</p>',
          content:
            '<p><span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> By mid-1934, Hitler faced a serious internal power struggle. Ernst Röhm, commander of the three-million-strong SA, demanded a \'second revolution\' to redistribute wealth and absorb the aristocratic regular army (Reichswehr) into his brownshirt militia. The traditional officer corps and Defence Minister Werner von Blomberg were horrified, warning Hitler that President Hindenburg would declare martial law unless the unruly SA was brought under control.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> Prompted by fabricated dossiers of an impending SA coup compiled by Heinrich Himmler and Reinhard Heydrich, Hitler struck on 30 June 1934, known as the Night of the Long Knives. SS execution squads arrested Röhm and senior SA commanders at a Bavarian resort, shooting them without trial. In Berlin, hit squads assassinated former Chancellor Kurt von Schleicher and prominent dissident Gregor Strasser, eliminating internal party radicalism and securing the military\'s gratitude.</p>',
