          title:
            "Swastika Robes: Nazifying the Judiciary and Roland Freisler's People’s Court (1933–1934)",
          act: 3,
          text: "<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> To ensure the legal system served as an obedient weapon of repression, Hitler subordinated the German judiciary to Nazi ideology. All judges and lawyers were required to join the National Socialist League for the Maintenance of the Law (BSRB), swearing an oath to 'follow the directives of the Führer as the highest source of law.' Traditional impartial justice was dismantled: judges wore swastika emblems on their robes and based verdicts on 'sound popular sentiment' (gesundes Volksempfinden) and racial state interests.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> In 1934, following Hitler's fury that the Supreme Court had acquitted three communists accused in the Reichstag fire, the regime created the Volksgerichtshof (People’s Court). Designed specifically to try political crimes such as treason and sabotaging public morale, trials were held in secret without juries, and defendants were denied independent counsel. Presided over by fanatical Nazi judges like Roland Freisler, the People's Court handed down thousands of death sentences, turning the law into an engine of state murder.</p>",
          content:
            "<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> To ensure the legal system served as an obedient weapon of repression, Hitler subordinated the German judiciary to Nazi ideology. All judges and lawyers were required to join the National Socialist League for the Maintenance of the Law (BSRB), swearing an oath to 'follow the directives of the Führer as the highest source of law.' Traditional impartial justice was dismantled: judges wore swastika emblems on their robes and based verdicts on 'sound popular sentiment' (gesundes Volksempfinden) and racial state interests.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> In 1934, following Hitler's fury that the Supreme Court had acquitted three communists accused in the Reichstag fire, the regime created the Volksgerichtshof (People’s Court). Designed specifically to try political crimes such as treason and sabotaging public morale, trials were held in secret without juries, and defendants were denied independent counsel. Presided over by fanatical Nazi judges like Roland Freisler, the People's Court handed down thousands of death sentences, turning the law into an engine of state murder.</p>",
