          act: 4,
          text: "<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> In July 1933, Hitler sought international respectability by signing the Reichskonkordat with the Holy See, negotiated by Franz von Papen and Cardinal Eugenio Pacelli. Under the treaty, the Vatican agreed that German Catholic priests would withdraw from active politics and that the Catholic Centre Party would dissolve. In exchange, the Nazi government pledged to guarantee religious freedom for Germany's 22 million Catholics, maintain Catholic schools, and protect Catholic youth groups and charitable institutions from state interference.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> The treaty was broken almost immediately by the regime. Catholic youth organizations were forcibly disbanded to funnel boys into the Hitler Youth, crucifixes were stripped from classroom walls, and priests faced arrest in staged 'morality trials'. In March 1937, Pope Pius XI smuggled his famous encyclical, Mit Brennender Sorge ('With Burning Concern'), into Germany. Read from every Catholic pulpit, it condemned Nazi racial idolatry and state terror, provoking intensified Gestapo arrests and harassment of Catholic clergy.</p>",
