            "Pagan Crosses vs Confessional Pulpits: Ludwig Müller's Reich Church and Martin Niemöller's Resistance (1933–1939)",
          act: 5,
          text: "<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Germany's 40 million Protestants were initially viewed by Hitler as natural allies due to their historical patriotism and hostility toward communism. In 1933, the regime coordinated 28 regional Protestant bodies into a unified Reich Church (Reichskirche) led by Hitler's handpicked Reich Bishop, Ludwig Müller. Supported by the fanatical 'German Christians' (Deutsche Christen) movement, Müller attempted to Nazify Christianity, removing the Jewish Old Testament from scriptures and placing swastikas alongside crucifixes on church altars.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> This pagan corruption triggered fierce theological resistance. In September 1933, Pastor Martin Niemöller and Dietrich Bonhoeffer formed the Pastors' Emergency League, which evolved into the Confessing Church (Bekennende Kirche) with over 6,000 pastors adhering to the 1934 Barmen Declaration. Niemöller openly declared that 'we must obey God rather than man', refusing state control over doctrine. In response, the Gestapo arrested over 800 Confessing pastors, sending Niemöller himself to Sachsenhausen and Dachau from 1937 until 1945.</p>",
