          title:
            'The Ministry of Public Enlightenment: Joseph Goebbels and the Reich Chamber of Culture (1933)',
          act: 1,
          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> In March 1933, Hitler created the Ministry for Public Enlightenment and Propaganda (RMVP), appointing 35-year-old Dr. Joseph Goebbels as its minister. Goebbels recognized that naked terror alone was insufficient to sustain dictatorship; public attitudes had to be systematically coordinated into enthusiastic conformity. Operating from Berlin, Goebbels defined his task as ensuring that \'all people think uniformly, react uniformly, and place themselves at the disposal of the government with all their soul\', establishing total state control over mass communications.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> In September 1933, Goebbels established the Reichskulturkammer (Reich Chamber of Culture), dividing cultural life into seven compulsory chambers: press, radio, film, literature, theater, music, and fine arts. Every artist, author, musician, and journalist had to register with their respective chamber; Jews, socialists, and modernists were excluded, ending their livelihoods. Anyone producing unapproved art faced immediate Gestapo arrest, ensuring that German cultural expression was completely aligned with Aryan racial nationalism.</p>',
