              context:
                "William L. Shirer's eyewitness observation of mass hysteria at the 1934 Nuremberg Party Rally.",
              question_badge: 'How Useful (Paper 3 Q3a)',
              question_type: 'How Useful (Paper 3 Q3a)',
              enquiry_question:
                'How useful is Source C for an enquiry into the psychological appeal and effectiveness of Nazi mass rallies in the 1930s?',
              model_answer:
                '<p><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source C is exceptionally useful because it provides an astute contemporary analysis of the psychological mechanisms underpinning Nazi mass spectacle. Shirer observes that the rallies succeeded not through logical persuasion, but by tapping into a quasi-religious desire for redemption following the trauma of the 1918 defeat and the Great Depression. His description of Speer\'s "cathedral of light" and the acoustic hypnosis created by 100,000 voices chanting in unison corroborates how the regime deliberately overwhelmed individual critical reason to produce collective hysteria and unconditional obedience.</p><p><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> The utility is further elevated by the source\'s provenance as the private diary of an experienced, politically independent American foreign correspondent living in Berlin. Unlike official Nazi newsreels directed by Leni Riefenstahl, Shirer had no obligation to glorify Hitler, and his admission that even an outsider felt the "magnetic pull of the herd" testifies to the genuine sensory power of the staging. However, its utility must be qualified by recognising that Shirer was observing a handpicked audience of fanatical party loyalists assembled in Nuremberg; his impressions cannot be generalised to prove that all ordinary Germans, particularly sceptical industrial workers or religious dissenters listening on the radio at home, shared this uncritical adulation.</p>',
