          title:
            'Pins Under the Lapel: The Edelweiss Pirates and Working-Class Youth Defiance (1936–1939)',
          act: 3,
          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> As the regime made Hitler Youth membership compulsory in 1936 and intensified military drills, rebellious teenagers formed counter-cultural protest groups. In working-class industrial cities like Cologne, Düsseldorf, and Essen, adolescents formed the Edelweiss Pirates (Edelweisspiraten). Choosing the alpine edelweiss flower badge pinned beneath their collars as an emblem, they wore distinctive check shirts, dark trousers, and scarves, deliberately violating the drab uniform regulations of the Hitler Youth.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> The Pirates gathered in parks and riverbanks, singing satirical parodies of Nazi songs and camping in the countryside to escape state surveillance. Their defiance frequently turned physical: groups ambushed Hitler Youth patrols, attacked local Nazi officials, and daubed anti-war slogans like \'Medals for Murder\' on railway carriages. While primarily an anti-authoritarian youth rebellion rather than a conspiracy to overthrow Hitler, the regime responded with public beatings, Gestapo detention, and forced shaved heads.</p>',
          content:
            '<p><span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> As the regime made Hitler Youth membership compulsory in 1936 and intensified military drills, rebellious teenagers formed counter-cultural protest groups. In working-class industrial cities like Cologne, Düsseldorf, and Essen, adolescents formed the Edelweiss Pirates (Edelweisspiraten). Choosing the alpine edelweiss flower badge pinned beneath their collars as an emblem, they wore distinctive check shirts, dark trousers, and scarves, deliberately violating the drab uniform regulations of the Hitler Youth.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> The Pirates gathered in parks and riverbanks, singing satirical parodies of Nazi songs and camping in the countryside to escape state surveillance. Their defiance frequently turned physical: groups ambushed Hitler Youth patrols, attacked local Nazi officials, and daubed anti-war slogans like \'Medals for Murder\' on railway carriages. While primarily an anti-authoritarian youth rebellion rather than a conspiracy to overthrow Hitler, the regime responded with public beatings, Gestapo detention, and forced shaved heads.</p>',
