          act: 4,
          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> In contrast to the working-class Edelweiss Pirates, the Swing Youth (Swingjugend) emerged among wealthy, middle-class teenagers in cosmopolitan centers such as Hamburg, Berlin, and Frankfurt. Passionately devoted to American and British swing jazz—music the RMVP officially banned as \'degenerate negro music\'—these youths formed private clubs and dance halls. They embraced Anglo-American fashion, with boys growing long hair and wearing English tweed jackets, while girls wore lipstick and short skirts.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> The Swing Youth rejected the militaristic regimentation and racial fanaticism of the Nazi state, greeting each other with sarcastic shouts of \'Swing Heil!\' and dancing uninhibitedly to records by Benny Goodman and Duke Ellington. Although they lacked an overt political ideology, Heinrich Himmler viewed their counter-culture as subversive treason that corrupted Aryan youth. In 1938, the Gestapo launched raids on jazz clubs, arresting ringleaders and transferring repeat offenders to youth concentration camps like Moringen.</p>',
          content:
            '<p><span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> In contrast to the working-class Edelweiss Pirates, the Swing Youth (Swingjugend) emerged among wealthy, middle-class teenagers in cosmopolitan centers such as Hamburg, Berlin, and Frankfurt. Passionately devoted to American and British swing jazz—music the RMVP officially banned as \'degenerate negro music\'—these youths formed private clubs and dance halls. They embraced Anglo-American fashion, with boys growing long hair and wearing English tweed jackets, while girls wore lipstick and short skirts.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> The Swing Youth rejected the militaristic regimentation and racial fanaticism of the Nazi state, greeting each other with sarcastic shouts of \'Swing Heil!\' and dancing uninhibitedly to records by Benny Goodman and Duke Ellington. Although they lacked an overt political ideology, Heinrich Himmler viewed their counter-culture as subversive treason that corrupted Aryan youth. In 1938, the Gestapo launched raids on jazz clubs, arresting ringleaders and transferring repeat offenders to youth concentration camps like Moringen.</p>',
