          title:
            'The Lion of Münster: Bishop von Galen, Dietrich Bonhoeffer and Religious Courage (1937–1939)',
          act: 5,
          text: "<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Religious figures provided the most influential challenge to Nazi authority because the Christian churches retained deep institutional roots and moral credibility. In Münster, the Catholic Bishop Clemens August Graf von Galen earned the nickname 'The Lion of Münster' by delivering blistering sermons attacking Gestapo brutality and the confiscation of church property. Galen's sermons were circulated widely, proving so popular among soldiers that Hitler hesitated to execute him during wartime for fear of causing rebellion in Westphalia.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Among Protestants, Dietrich Bonhoeffer emerged as the moral conscience of the Confessing Church. Bonhoeffer organized illegal underground seminaries for young pastors at Finkenwalde, preaching that genuine Christians could not remain silent while their Jewish neighbours were persecuted, arguing that 'silence in the face of evil is itself evil.' Bonhoeffer's network forged contacts with anti-Nazi officers in the military intelligence (Abwehr), transforming theological dissent into an active conspiracy against Hitler's regime.</p>",
