          title: 'The Weimar Contrast and the Nazi Ideal of Womanhood',
          act: 1,
          text: "<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> During the Weimar Republic, German women experienced unprecedented emancipation. Article 109 of the 1919 constitution guaranteed female suffrage and equal rights, with over one hundred women elected to the Reichstag. Modern 'New Women' (Neue Frau) entered teaching, medicine, and commerce, sporting short hair and social independence. To Adolf Hitler and conservatives, however, this liberation was a decadent threat that had caused annual births to fall from over two million in 1900 to under one million by 1933.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Nazi ideology reversed female emancipation under the slogan Kinder, Küche, Kirche (Children, Kitchen, Church). Women were defined not as wage earners, but as biological mothers duty-bound to bear racially pure Aryan soldiers. Gertrud Scholtz-Klink was appointed Reich Women's Leader, consolidating all female associations into the National Socialist Women's League (NS-Frauenschaft). Through home-economics courses and rallies, Scholtz-Klink insisted that women's primary role was selflessly serving the national community (Volksgemeinschaft).</p>",
