          title: 'Eugenic Control, Sterilisation, and the Lebensborn Program',
          act: 4,
          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> Nazi family policy was governed by pseudo-scientific racial hygiene, restricting motherhood strictly to those deemed \'genetically valuable\'. In July 1933, the Law for the Prevention of Hereditarily Diseased Offspring authorized compulsory surgical sterilisation for conditions such as hereditary blindness, schizophrenia, epilepsy, or severe alcoholism. Over 400,000 Germans—mostly young women—were sterilised under verdicts from secret Genetic Health Courts. Furthermore, the 1935 Marital Health Law required couples to provide medical proof of racial and genetic fitness before marrying.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> For racially pristine Aryan women, Heinrich Himmler\'s SS founded the Lebensborn (Fount of Life) program in 1935. Initially providing discreet maternity homes for unmarried mothers of pure ancestry, Lebensborn quickly evolved into an elite demographic experiment. Unmarried women who proved four Aryan grandparents were encouraged to mate with SS officers in state clinics to produce children for the master race. Approximately 8,000 children were born in Lebensborn homes, prioritizing racial expansion over conventional Christian marriage.</p>',
