          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[3.1]</span> Parallel to the militarization of boys, German girls were organized into gender-segregated movements to prepare them for domestic motherhood. Girls aged ten to fourteen joined the Young Girls (Jungmädelbund), before entering the League of German Girls (Bund Deutscher Mädel, or BDM) from age fourteen to eighteen. Between eighteen and twenty-one, young women could join \'Faith and Beauty\' (Glaube und Schönheit), which bridged the transition from adolescent training to adult membership in the Women\'s League.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[3.2]</span> BDM members wore uniform blue skirts, white blouses, brown suede jackets, and black neckerchiefs, removing social class distinctions. While activities included camping, choral singing, and racial ideology classes, physical fitness was paramount. Girls performed gymnastics, callisthenics, and folk dancing to build strong bodies capable of bearing healthy Aryan children. During holidays, BDM members were mobilized for harvest work (Landdienst), assisting rural farming families while receiving instruction in cooking, infant hygiene, and domestic nursing.</p>',
