            '<p><strong>Content &amp; Contextual Knowledge:</strong> Source B is extremely useful because it provides an authoritative statement of the regime’s fundamental aims for German boys directly from Hitler himself. His famous slogan that boys must be "swift as a greyhound, tough as leather, and hard as Krupp steel" demonstrates that education was subordinated to military preparation and physical endurance rather than intellectual scholarship. This was enacted in practice: Education Minister Bernhard Rust allocated 15% of all school timetables to PE, while the Hitler Youth focused on compass reading, trench digging, rifle firing, and weekend route marches to prepare boys for military conscription after 1935.</p><p><strong>Provenance &amp; Utility Evaluation:</strong> The utility is heightened by its provenance as a live rally speech delivered to 54,000 uniformed adolescents at Nuremberg. This shows how mass theatrical spectacles were used to create emotional fanaticism and demand "unconditional obedience" to the Führer. However, its utility is limited because it portrays an idealized propaganda image of total devotion. It does not reflect the growing boredom, exhaustion, and alienation felt by many boys as the 1930s progressed, nor does it acknowledge the emergence of active youth resistance groups like the Edelweiss Pirates who rejected this militarism.</p>',
