          text: '<p><span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> In 1933, the regime launched an immediate campaign to ostracize Germany\'s 500,000 Jews—under one percent of the population. On 1 April 1933, the state staged a nationwide boycott of Jewish shops, doctors, and lawyers. Uniformed SA stormtroopers guarded entrances, painting yellow Stars of David and slogans on windows while intimidating shoppers. Though halted after one day due to international criticism, the boycott signaled the regime\'s determination to drive Jews out of economic life.</p>\n\n<p><span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> On 7 April 1933, the Law for the Restoration of the Professional Civil Service dismissed Jewish civil servants, judges, professors, and teachers. Over the next two years, Jewish doctors were barred from treating non-Jewish patients, lawyers were denied court licenses, and journalists were purged under the Reich Press Law. Through escalating decrees, the state systematically stripped Jewish citizens of professional livelihoods and social standing, forcing them into civic isolation.</p>',
